turns.text:
Because the object prop is probably getting a new reference on every parent render.

In React, prop comparison is by reference, not by deep value. So these two objects are "different" even if they contain the same fields:

```js
{ a: 1 } !== { a: 1 }
```

If your parent does this on every render:

```jsx
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />
```

then `Child` receives a brand new object each time. That defeats `React.memo` shallow comparison and can also retrigger effects that depend on that object.

Use a stable reference instead:

```jsx
const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme: 'dark' }), []);
return <Child config={config} />;
```

Or pass primitives instead of an object when possible:

```jsx
<Child theme="dark" />
```

Also note:
- A child normally re-renders whenever its parent re-renders unless you memoize the child with `React.memo`.
- `React.memo` only helps if the prop references stay stable.

Typical fix:

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ config }) {
  return <div>{config.theme}</div>;
});

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme: 'dark' }), []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>{count}</button>
      <Child config={config} />
    </>
  );
}
```

